  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'The orientation of a plane in space is expressed by its attitude, a term consisting of two components, strike and dip. Define strike and dip with proper example and illustration.', 'Strike is the compass direction (bearing) of the line formed by the intersection of an inclined plane (bed, fault, joint, foliation) with an imaginary horizontal plane. It is a horizontal line on the plane and is measured from north, e.g. N45°E (or 045°).
Dip is the angle of maximum inclination of the plane below the horizontal, measured in the vertical plane perpendicular to the strike, together with the direction in which the plane slopes down (dip direction), e.g. 30° towards SE. Any dip measured in another direction (apparent dip) is smaller than the true dip.
Example: a sandstone bed striking N45°E and dipping 30° towards the south-east is written N45°E/30°SE (or as dip direction/dip, 135/30). Strike and dip are measured in the field with a Brunton or clinometer compass and shown on maps by a T-shaped symbol (long line = strike, short tick = dip direction, with the dip angle).
Illustration: a block diagram showing an inclined bed, a horizontal plane cutting it along the strike line, an arrow perpendicular to strike pointing down-slope (dip direction) and the angle between the bed and the horizontal (dip angle).', null, '[{"point": "Strike: direction (bearing) of the line of intersection of the inclined plane with a horizontal plane, measured from north, e.g. N45°E or 045°", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Dip: maximum angle of inclination of the plane below the horizontal, measured perpendicular to strike, with dip direction, e.g. 30° towards SE; apparent dip is less", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Example/ illustration: block diagram showing strike line, dip angle and direction; notation e.g. N45°E/30°SE or 135/30; measured with Brunton/ clinometer compass; map symbol", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
