  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Differentiate between weathering and erosion. Provide examples of each process.', 'Weathering is the breaking down or chemical alteration of rocks and minerals in place (in situ), at or near the Earth''s surface, by contact with the atmosphere, water and organisms - there is no significant movement of the material. Examples: physical weathering such as frost wedging (water freezing in cracks, common at high altitude in Bhutan), exfoliation and thermal expansion; chemical weathering such as oxidation of iron minerals (rusting), hydrolysis of feldspar to clay and carbonation/solution of limestone; biological weathering by plant roots and lichens.
Erosion is the removal and transport of the weathered material from one place to another by agents such as running water, glaciers, wind, waves and gravity. Examples: rivers cutting valleys and gorges and carrying sediment, gully erosion on bare slopes, glaciers carving U-shaped valleys, wind removing dust, and landslides and debris flows moving material downslope.
Difference: weathering breaks rock down without moving it; erosion moves the products. Weathering usually prepares material for erosion, and both together shape the landscape.', null, '[{"point": "Weathering: in-situ breakdown/ decomposition of rocks at or near surface without significant transport; physical (frost wedging, exfoliation, thermal), chemical (oxidation, hydrolysis, carbonation/ solution of limestone), biological (roots, lichens)", "marks": 2.5}, {"point": "Erosion: removal and transport of weathered material by agents - running water, glaciers, wind, waves, gravity (mass wasting); examples gullies, river valleys/ gorges, glacial valleys, landslides; weathering prepares material, erosion moves it", "marks": 2.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
