  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Explain what you understand by the term foliation in metamorphic rocks and state its significance.', 'Foliation is the planar fabric in metamorphic rocks formed when platy or elongated minerals (micas, chlorite, amphiboles) grow or rotate parallel to each other, or when minerals segregate into light and dark bands, under directed pressure (differential stress) during metamorphism - usually regional metamorphism in mountain belts. The foliation forms roughly perpendicular to the direction of maximum compression.
Types, with increasing metamorphic grade: slaty cleavage (slate), phyllitic texture (phyllite), schistosity (schist) and gneissic banding (gneiss). Rocks formed without directed stress, such as marble and quartzite, are non-foliated.
Significance: (1) it helps classify and name metamorphic rocks and indicates the metamorphic grade; (2) it records the direction of stress and the deformation history, useful in structural mapping (e.g. in the Himalaya); (3) foliation planes are planes of weakness along which rocks split, so they control slope stability, landslides, rock falls, tunnelling, excavation and foundation design - slopes where foliation dips out of the slope are unstable; (4) they affect permeability and groundwater flow; (5) practical use - slate splits along cleavage into roofing slabs.', null, '[{"point": "Foliation: planar, layered/ parallel arrangement of platy/ elongated minerals (mica, chlorite, amphibole) or banding produced by directed pressure during (regional) metamorphism, perpendicular to maximum stress", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Types with increasing grade: slaty cleavage (slate), phyllitic, schistosity (schist), gneissic banding (gneiss); non-foliated: marble, quartzite", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Significance: indicates direction of stress and metamorphic grade/ deformation history, used in rock classification; plane of weakness - affects slope stability, tunnelling, landslides, foundations, permeability; slate splits for roofing", "marks": 2}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'geology'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 5,
    540, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
