  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case I: Preliminary landslide study
Landslides are one of the most significant natural hazards affecting mountainous and hilly terrains globally, affecting the environment, infrastructure and human safety. Bhutan, located in the eastern Himalayas, has rugged topography, fragile geology and heavy precipitation, especially during the monsoon, and faces tremendous challenges from landslides.
Scenario: A major landslide has occurred in the southern part of the country, an area of mixed soil and rock types of the Lesser Himalaya with steep and rugged topography and heavy monsoon rainfall averaging over 4000 mm per year. The landslide has caused significant damage to local infrastructure, including roads and residential areas. As a geologist, you have been assigned to conduct a preliminary study of the landslide and prepare a preliminary report outlining the key findings and recommendations.
Information you may use: describe the site visit methodology and data collected (geological formations, soil properties, slope geometry, hydrological conditions), the extent of the landslide and affected infrastructure, vegetation and landscape changes; characterise the site (rock types, soils, slope angles, structures; rainfall, groundwater, seepage; vegetation and land use); analyse triggering factors (rainfall, seismic activity, slope stability, human activities), failure mechanisms and a qualitative stability assessment; and give preliminary recommendations (stabilisation, drainage, land use changes), immediate actions including further detailed studies, and long-term measures.
Write the following section of your report:

Landslide assessment: analysis of triggering factors and qualitative stability assessment. (15 marks)', 'Causative (preparatory) factors: (1) steep, rugged slopes; (2) weak Lesser Himalayan rocks - phyllite, schist and slate that weather deeply to clay and are highly fractured; (3) foliation and joints dipping out of the slope (adverse orientation), forming sliding planes; (4) thick, loose colluvium over bedrock; (5) closeness to major thrusts such as the Main Boundary Thrust, which crush the rocks; (6) toe cutting by the road and erosion by the stream; (7) poor surface and sub-surface drainage (blocked drains, uncontrolled road runoff); and (8) removal of vegetation.
Triggering factor: the main trigger was intense and prolonged monsoon rainfall (over 4000 mm per year, with heavy rain in the preceding days). Infiltration saturated the soil and weathered rock, raised groundwater and pore-water pressure, added weight and reduced effective shear strength, especially along the soil-rock contact. Earthquakes are a possible secondary trigger in this seismically active zone; human activities (road excavation, irrigation channels, blocked culverts) increased instability.
Failure mechanism: the features suggest a rotational to translational slide in the colluvium and weathered rock, with a planar component along foliation, which turned into a debris flow at the toe after saturation (Varnes classification: complex slide-flow).
Qualitative stability assessment: the slope is presently marginally stable to unstable (factor of safety close to 1): fresh tension cracks above the crown, the steep unsupported main scarp, continuing seepage and active toe erosion indicate that the slide may retrogress upslope and that further failures are likely in the coming rains. The hazard to the road and to houses near the crown and along the debris path is therefore high, and the site should be treated as an active landslide.', null, '[{"point": "Preparatory (causal) factors: steep slope, weak, weathered/ fractured phyllite-schist, foliation/ joints dipping out of slope, thick colluvium, proximity to MBT/ MCT faults, toe cutting by road/ stream, deforestation, poor drainage", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Triggering factor: intense/ prolonged monsoon rainfall raising pore water pressure, saturating soil, reducing shear strength; possibly earthquake; human activity (excavation, blocked drains, irrigation)", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Failure mechanism: rotational/ translational slide in soil-weathered rock, planar slide along foliation, transforming into debris flow; type classification (Varnes)", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Qualitative stability: currently marginally stable/ unstable - fresh cracks above crown, seepage, steep scarp, active toe erosion - risk of retrogression and further failure in rains; hazard ranking high; Factor of Safety near 1", "marks": 3}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'geology'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 15,
    1620, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (Technical, Geology): BCSE 2024, batch 1", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2024 Main Examination, Technical Paper III, Geology", "year": 2024, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/technical-paper-iii/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-technical-3-geo2024-case1', 3, null, null, (select id from public.specialisations where code = 'geology'), null)
