  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case I: Preliminary landslide study
Landslides are one of the most significant natural hazards affecting mountainous and hilly terrains globally, affecting the environment, infrastructure and human safety. Bhutan, located in the eastern Himalayas, has rugged topography, fragile geology and heavy precipitation, especially during the monsoon, and faces tremendous challenges from landslides.
Scenario: A major landslide has occurred in the southern part of the country, an area of mixed soil and rock types of the Lesser Himalaya with steep and rugged topography and heavy monsoon rainfall averaging over 4000 mm per year. The landslide has caused significant damage to local infrastructure, including roads and residential areas. As a geologist, you have been assigned to conduct a preliminary study of the landslide and prepare a preliminary report outlining the key findings and recommendations.
Information you may use: describe the site visit methodology and data collected (geological formations, soil properties, slope geometry, hydrological conditions), the extent of the landslide and affected infrastructure, vegetation and landscape changes; characterise the site (rock types, soils, slope angles, structures; rainfall, groundwater, seepage; vegetation and land use); analyse triggering factors (rainfall, seismic activity, slope stability, human activities), failure mechanisms and a qualitative stability assessment; and give preliminary recommendations (stabilisation, drainage, land use changes), immediate actions including further detailed studies, and long-term measures.
Write the following section of your report:

Recommendations: suggested measures for immediate (including additional detailed studies) and long-term mitigation measures. (10 marks)', 'Immediate measures: keep people out of the danger zone (relocate households at the crown and in the debris path), put up warning signs and restrict traffic with a safe temporary road diversion; divert surface water away from the slide with temporary lined drains, clear blocked road drains and culverts, cover open tension cracks with plastic sheets; begin simple monitoring - crack pegs, survey points, a rain gauge - with a community early-warning arrangement for heavy rainfall.
Additional detailed studies: detailed topographic survey (drone/total station); subsurface investigation with boreholes, test pits and geophysics (electrical resistivity, seismic refraction) to find the depth of the slip surface and bedrock; laboratory testing of soil and rock (shear strength, index properties); installation of piezometers and inclinometers to monitor groundwater and movement; quantitative slope stability analysis (limit equilibrium/numerical, factor of safety under saturated and seismic conditions); hazard and risk mapping and rainfall thresholds for early warning.
Long-term mitigation: comprehensive surface drainage (catch drains above the crown, lined cascades) and sub-surface drainage (horizontal drains, trench drains) to lower groundwater; slope re-profiling and benching to reduce slope angle and load at the crown; retaining structures (gabion and reinforced concrete walls), toe protection and check dams in the stream; soil nailing or rock anchors where needed; bio-engineering with grasses, shrubs and native trees and reforestation of the catchment; land-use zoning that avoids construction in high-hazard zones and relocation of exposed houses; possible road realignment; and regular maintenance and long-term monitoring.', null, '[{"point": "Immediate: evacuate/ restrict access to danger zone, warning signs, monitoring (cracks, pegs, rain gauge), divert surface water/ seal cracks with tarpaulin, clear blocked drains, temporary road diversion, community early warning", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Further detailed studies: topographic survey/ drone, boreholes and geophysics (ERT, seismic refraction), lab tests (shear strength), groundwater monitoring (piezometers), inclinometers, quantitative slope stability analysis (FoS, numerical), hazard/ risk mapping, rainfall thresholds", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Long-term: surface and subsurface drainage (catch drains, horizontal drains), slope regrading/ benching, retaining structures (gabions, RCC walls), toe protection/ check dams, soil nails/ anchors, bioengineering and reforestation, land-use zoning/ relocation, road realignment, regular maintenance and monitoring", "marks": 4}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'geology'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (Technical, Geology): BCSE 2024, batch 1", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2024 Main Examination, Technical Paper III, Geology", "year": 2024, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/technical-paper-iii/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-technical-3-geo2024-case1', 4, null, null, (select id from public.specialisations where code = 'geology'), null)
