  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case I: Preliminary landslide study
Landslides are one of the most significant natural hazards affecting mountainous and hilly terrains globally, affecting the environment, infrastructure and human safety. Bhutan, located in the eastern Himalayas, has rugged topography, fragile geology and heavy precipitation, especially during the monsoon, and faces tremendous challenges from landslides.
Scenario: A major landslide has occurred in the southern part of the country, an area of mixed soil and rock types of the Lesser Himalaya with steep and rugged topography and heavy monsoon rainfall averaging over 4000 mm per year. The landslide has caused significant damage to local infrastructure, including roads and residential areas. As a geologist, you have been assigned to conduct a preliminary study of the landslide and prepare a preliminary report outlining the key findings and recommendations.
Information you may use: describe the site visit methodology and data collected (geological formations, soil properties, slope geometry, hydrological conditions), the extent of the landslide and affected infrastructure, vegetation and landscape changes; characterise the site (rock types, soils, slope angles, structures; rainfall, groundwater, seepage; vegetation and land use); analyse triggering factors (rainfall, seismic activity, slope stability, human activities), failure mechanisms and a qualitative stability assessment; and give preliminary recommendations (stabilisation, drainage, land use changes), immediate actions including further detailed studies, and long-term measures.
Write the following section of your report:

Limitations of the study: point out the important limitations of the study and suggest means to address these shortcomings. (5 marks)', 'Limitations: the study was preliminary and based mainly on a short field visit and visual observation; parts of the slide (the scarp and toe) could not be accessed safely; there was no subsurface information (depth of slip surface, groundwater level) and no laboratory testing of soil and rock strength, so stability could only be judged qualitatively; long-term rainfall, groundwater and seismic data near the site were limited; high-resolution pre-event topographic maps were not available; and continuing monsoon rain limited field work.
Addressing them: carry out a detailed investigation with boreholes, test pits and geophysical surveys; laboratory testing of samples; install instruments (piezometers, inclinometers, extensometers, rain gauges) and monitor through at least one full monsoon; use drone and satellite remote sensing (InSAR) for high-resolution topography and movement; obtain rainfall and seismic data from NCHM and DGM; and involve a multidisciplinary team (geologists, geotechnical and civil engineers, hydrologists).', null, '[{"point": "Limitations: short field time/ preliminary visual assessment, unsafe access to parts of slide, no subsurface data, no lab testing, limited rainfall/ groundwater/ seismic records, lack of high-resolution maps, monsoon weather", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Address: detailed investigation with boreholes/ geophysics, lab tests, instrumentation and monitoring over seasons, high-resolution survey/ remote sensing, multidisciplinary team, data from NCHM/ DGM", "marks": 2}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
