  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case I: Preliminary landslide study
Landslides are one of the most significant natural hazards affecting mountainous and hilly terrains globally, affecting the environment, infrastructure and human safety. Bhutan, located in the eastern Himalayas, has rugged topography, fragile geology and heavy precipitation, especially during the monsoon, and faces tremendous challenges from landslides.
Scenario: A major landslide has occurred in the southern part of the country, an area of mixed soil and rock types of the Lesser Himalaya with steep and rugged topography and heavy monsoon rainfall averaging over 4000 mm per year. The landslide has caused significant damage to local infrastructure, including roads and residential areas. As a geologist, you have been assigned to conduct a preliminary study of the landslide and prepare a preliminary report outlining the key findings and recommendations.
Information you may use: describe the site visit methodology and data collected (geological formations, soil properties, slope geometry, hydrological conditions), the extent of the landslide and affected infrastructure, vegetation and landscape changes; characterise the site (rock types, soils, slope angles, structures; rainfall, groundwater, seepage; vegetation and land use); analyse triggering factors (rainfall, seismic activity, slope stability, human activities), failure mechanisms and a qualitative stability assessment; and give preliminary recommendations (stabilisation, drainage, land use changes), immediate actions including further detailed studies, and long-term measures.
Write the following section of your report:

Conclusion: summary of key findings and implications for future studies or interventions. (5 marks)', 'The preliminary study shows that the landslide is a rotational-translational slide-debris flow in deeply weathered, fractured Lesser Himalayan phyllite and schist and the overlying colluvium. The slope was predisposed to failure by its steepness, weak rocks, foliation dipping out of the slope, thick soil cover, road cutting at the toe and poor drainage, and it was triggered by prolonged, intense monsoon rainfall that raised pore-water pressure. The slide remains active, with tension cracks above the crown, so the risk to the road and nearby houses is high.
Implications: immediate safety, drainage and monitoring measures are urgent; a detailed geotechnical investigation and quantitative stability analysis are needed before designing permanent stabilisation works; landslide hazard mapping, rainfall-based early warning and risk-informed land-use planning should be extended to similar slopes in southern Bhutan; and geological input should be built into the planning and maintenance of roads and settlements to reduce future landslide disasters.', null, '[{"point": "Summary: slide caused by steep slope, weak weathered Lesser Himalayan rocks and adverse structure, triggered by heavy monsoon rain and pore pressure, worsened by road cutting/ poor drainage; still active and high risk", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Implications: urgent safety and drainage measures, detailed investigation before design of permanent works, hazard mapping and land-use planning, early warning, integrate geological input in road/ settlement planning in southern Bhutan", "marks": 2}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'geology'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 5,
    540, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
