  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case II: The Himalayas - formation, geology and mineral potential of Bhutan
The Himalayas, one of the most prominent and geologically significant mountain ranges in the world, have a complex formation history and play a crucial role in regional geology and tectonics. You are tasked to prepare a report that explains the formation of the Himalayas, describes their geological and tectonic features, and discusses their potential for mineral resources in the Kingdom of Bhutan.
Information you may use: the geological processes and plate tectonics (collision of the Indian and Eurasian plates); the timeline from initial plate movements to the present, including major uplift phases and faulting; the major geological formations, units and rock types of the Bhutan Himalaya; structural features and major tectonic faults; current tectonic activity (seismicity, ongoing deformation); and mineral resource potential - metallic (copper, tungsten, gold, lead-zinc) and industrial minerals (talc, limestone, dolomite), known deposits, exploration, economic potential and challenges in Bhutan.
Write the following section of your report:

Introduction: overview of the Himalayas and objectives of the report. (5 marks)', 'The Himalayas are the youngest and highest mountain range on Earth, forming an arc about 2400 km long from Nanga Parbat in the west to Namche Barwa in the east, with the world''s highest peaks including Mount Everest. They were formed by the collision of the Indian Plate with the Eurasian Plate and are still rising and seismically active. They control the climate of South Asia (monsoon barrier), are the source of major rivers, and contain important mineral, water and hydropower resources. Bhutan lies in the eastern Himalaya, where all the main tectonic zones of the range are exposed.
Objectives of this report: (1) explain the geological processes and timeline of Himalayan formation; (2) describe the geological units, rock types, structures and present tectonic activity of the Bhutan Himalaya; (3) assess the mineral resource potential and exploration status in Bhutan; and (4) point out limitations and directions for future research and resource development.', null, '[{"point": "Overview: youngest, highest mountain range, ~2400 km arc from Nanga Parbat to Namche Barwa, highest peaks (Everest), formed by India-Eurasia collision, still rising and seismically active; Bhutan in eastern Himalaya", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Objectives: explain formation and timeline, describe geology/ tectonics of Bhutan Himalaya, assess mineral potential, identify limitations and future work", "marks": 2}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'geology'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 5,
    540, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (Technical, Geology): BCSE 2024, batch 1", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2024 Main Examination, Technical Paper III, Geology", "year": 2024, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/technical-paper-iii/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-technical-3-geo2024-case2', 1, null, null, (select id from public.specialisations where code = 'geology'), null)
