  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case II: The Himalayas - formation, geology and mineral potential of Bhutan
The Himalayas, one of the most prominent and geologically significant mountain ranges in the world, have a complex formation history and play a crucial role in regional geology and tectonics. You are tasked to prepare a report that explains the formation of the Himalayas, describes their geological and tectonic features, and discusses their potential for mineral resources in the Kingdom of Bhutan.
Information you may use: the geological processes and plate tectonics (collision of the Indian and Eurasian plates); the timeline from initial plate movements to the present, including major uplift phases and faulting; the major geological formations, units and rock types of the Bhutan Himalaya; structural features and major tectonic faults; current tectonic activity (seismicity, ongoing deformation); and mineral resource potential - metallic (copper, tungsten, gold, lead-zinc) and industrial minerals (talc, limestone, dolomite), known deposits, exploration, economic potential and challenges in Bhutan.
Write the following section of your report:

Formation of the Himalayas: explanation of the geological processes and timeline of the Himalayas'' formation. (10 marks)', 'Geological processes: India was part of the southern supercontinent Gondwana. After Gondwana broke up, the Indian Plate drifted rapidly northward (up to 15-20 cm per year), while the oceanic crust of the Neo-Tethys Ocean in front of it subducted beneath the southern margin of Eurasia, producing an Andean-type volcanic arc and the Trans-Himalayan (Gangdese) batholith. When the ocean closed, the continental crust of India collided with Eurasia; because continental crust is too buoyant to subduct deeply, the northern edge of India was scraped off, folded, stacked into great thrust sheets, thickened, metamorphosed and uplifted, while Tibet was raised into a high plateau. Deformation then propagated southward over time on successively younger thrust faults.
Timeline: India rifted from Gondwana about 130-90 million years ago (Ma); initial collision began about 50-55 Ma (Eocene), marked by the Indus-Tsangpo Suture Zone; crustal thickening and metamorphism followed, with the Main Central Thrust (MCT) and the South Tibetan Detachment active about 23-15 Ma (Miocene), producing high-grade Greater Himalayan rocks and leucogranites; the Main Boundary Thrust (MBT) became active about 10-5 Ma, and the Siwalik foothills were folded along the Main Frontal Thrust (MFT) in the last few million years, which is still active today. India continues to move north at about 4-5 cm per year, with about 2 cm/yr absorbed across the Himalaya, so the mountains are still rising and earthquakes continue.', null, '[{"point": "Processes: break-up of Gondwana, India drifting north, subduction of Neo-Tethys oceanic crust beneath Eurasia (Andean-type margin, Trans-Himalayan batholith), continent-continent collision, crustal thickening, folding, thrusting, metamorphism, uplift", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Timeline: India separates ~130-90 Ma, fast northward drift (~15 cm/ yr), initial collision ~50-55 Ma (Eocene), Tethys closed (Indus-Tsangpo Suture); MCT active ~23-15 Ma (Miocene) with leucogranites; MBT ~10-5 Ma; MFT (Siwaliks) active today; India still converging ~4-5 cm/ yr", "marks": 5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'geology'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (Technical, Geology): BCSE 2024, batch 1", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2024 Main Examination, Technical Paper III, Geology", "year": 2024, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/technical-paper-iii/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-technical-3-geo2024-case2', 2, null, null, (select id from public.specialisations where code = 'geology'), null)
  returning id
)
