  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case II: The Himalayas - formation, geology and mineral potential of Bhutan
The Himalayas, one of the most prominent and geologically significant mountain ranges in the world, have a complex formation history and play a crucial role in regional geology and tectonics. You are tasked to prepare a report that explains the formation of the Himalayas, describes their geological and tectonic features, and discusses their potential for mineral resources in the Kingdom of Bhutan.
Information you may use: the geological processes and plate tectonics (collision of the Indian and Eurasian plates); the timeline from initial plate movements to the present, including major uplift phases and faulting; the major geological formations, units and rock types of the Bhutan Himalaya; structural features and major tectonic faults; current tectonic activity (seismicity, ongoing deformation); and mineral resource potential - metallic (copper, tungsten, gold, lead-zinc) and industrial minerals (talc, limestone, dolomite), known deposits, exploration, economic potential and challenges in Bhutan.
Write the following section of your report:

Geology and tectonics of the Bhutan Himalayas: description of the geological units, rock types and structural features; analysis of tectonic processes, current activity and plate interactions. (15 marks)', 'Geological units (from south to north), separated by major north-dipping fault zones:
1. Siwalik Group (sub-Himalaya) - Miocene-Pliocene sandstone, siltstone, mudstone and conglomerate deposited by rivers in the foreland, exposed in the southern foothills (e.g. Samtse, Gelephu, Samdrup Jongkhar), thrust over the plains along the Main Frontal Thrust (MFT).
2. Lesser Himalayan Sequence - low-grade Proterozoic to Palaeozoic metasediments: phyllite, slate, quartzite, schist, dolomite and limestone, and the Gondwana and Buxa/Baxa groups (including coal, dolomite, limestone, talc), and the Daling-Shumar and Jaishidanda units; bounded below by the Main Boundary Thrust (MBT). These weak, weathered rocks are very landslide-prone.
3. Greater Himalayan Sequence - the high-grade crystalline core that covers most of Bhutan: gneisses, migmatites, schists, quartzites, marbles and Miocene leucogranites, thrust south over the Lesser Himalaya along the Main Central Thrust (MCT); it includes internal thrusts such as the Kakhtang Thrust.
4. Tethyan Himalayan Sequence - weakly metamorphosed to unmetamorphosed fossiliferous sedimentary rocks (limestone, shale, sandstone), found in the north and as klippen (outliers) resting on the Greater Himalaya (e.g. around Chekha, Tang Chu/Ura and Lingshi), separated by the South Tibetan Detachment System (a normal fault zone).
Structural features: the MFT, MBT and MCT are the main thrusts that stack the units like sheets, together with the South Tibetan Detachment, out-of-sequence thrusts, klippen, duplexes, large folds and pervasive foliation. They record over 500 km of crustal shortening and control topography, river courses, landslides, springs and mineralisation.
Tectonic processes and current activity: the Indian Plate continues to under-thrust Eurasia; GPS measurements show convergence of about 1.5-2 cm per year across the Bhutan Himalaya, accumulating strain on the Main Himalayan Thrust. Bhutan lies in a high seismic zone with frequent small and moderate earthquakes (e.g. the 2009 Mongar earthquake of about magnitude 6.1) and evidence of great past earthquakes (e.g. about 1714). The Shillong Plateau to the south takes up part of the shortening. Ongoing uplift combined with intense monsoon erosion produces deep valleys, steep slopes and high landslide and earthquake hazard.', null, '[{"point": "Units south to north: Siwalik Group (sandstone, shale, conglomerate - southern foothills) / MFT; Lesser Himalayan Sequence (phyllite, quartzite, slate, dolomite, limestone, Buxa/ Daling, Baxa, Diuri; Shumar, Jaishidanda) / MBT; Greater Himalayan Sequence (gneiss, migmatite, schist, leucogranites; most of Bhutan) / MCT; Tethyan Himalaya (sedimentary, fossiliferous, north/ klippen e.g. Chekha, Tang Chu) / STDS", "marks": 7}, {"point": "Structures: MFT, MBT, MCT, STD, Kakhtang thrust, klippen (Ura, Lingshi), folds, foliation, duplexes; significance - stacked thrust sheets, crustal shortening, structural control of landslides/ minerals", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Current activity: ongoing convergence ~2 cm/ yr across Bhutan Himalaya, moderate seismicity, paleo-earthquakes (1714 M8 event), Shillong Plateau effect, seismic zone, GPS/ seismic monitoring, uplift/ erosion", "marks": 4}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'geology'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 15,
    1620, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (Technical, Geology): BCSE 2024, batch 1", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2024 Main Examination, Technical Paper III, Geology", "year": 2024, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/technical-paper-iii/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-technical-3-geo2024-case2', 3, null, null, (select id from public.specialisations where code = 'geology'), null)
  returning id
