  values ('ME', 'passage', 'Case II: The Himalayas - formation, geology and mineral potential of Bhutan
The Himalayas, one of the most prominent and geologically significant mountain ranges in the world, have a complex formation history and play a crucial role in regional geology and tectonics. You are tasked to prepare a report that explains the formation of the Himalayas, describes their geological and tectonic features, and discusses their potential for mineral resources in the Kingdom of Bhutan.
Information you may use: the geological processes and plate tectonics (collision of the Indian and Eurasian plates); the timeline from initial plate movements to the present, including major uplift phases and faulting; the major geological formations, units and rock types of the Bhutan Himalaya; structural features and major tectonic faults; current tectonic activity (seismicity, ongoing deformation); and mineral resource potential - metallic (copper, tungsten, gold, lead-zinc) and industrial minerals (talc, limestone, dolomite), known deposits, exploration, economic potential and challenges in Bhutan.
Write the following section of your report:

Mineral resources potential: assessment of mineral resources potential and current exploration status. (10 marks)', 'Assessment of potential: the geology of Bhutan favours mainly industrial minerals. The Lesser Himalayan sequence and Buxa/Gondwana groups host large deposits of dolomite, limestone, gypsum, talc, quartzite, coal, marble and slate; the Greater Himalayan crystalline rocks and Miocene leucogranites have potential for tungsten, tin, beryl, rare metals, mica and gemstones; and occurrences of copper, lead-zinc, gold and tungsten are known in parts of the Lesser and Greater Himalaya, although most are small or not yet evaluated.
Known deposits and exploration status: dolomite in Samtse and Pemagatshel (mined and exported); gypsum at Khothakpa in Pemagatshel; limestone for cement in Samtse, Pemagatshel (Dungsum) and Gomdar; coal in Samdrup Jongkhar (Gondwana); talc in Samtse; quartzite used for ferro-silicon; marble, slate and stone quarries in several districts; and base metal (lead-zinc, copper) and tungsten occurrences under exploration. The Department of Geology and Mines (DGM, now under the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources) carries out geological mapping and exploration; mining is regulated under the Mines and Minerals Management Act, and state-owned enterprises (e.g. State Mining Corporation Limited) now operate strategic mines in line with the Mineral Development Policy.
Economic potential and challenges: minerals support cement, ferro-alloy and other industries, exports, revenue and employment, and could diversify the economy beyond hydropower. Challenges include rugged terrain and poor access raising extraction and transport costs; small or poorly known deposits and limited detailed exploration data; environmental and social impacts (forest loss, dust, water pollution, landslides), high forest cover and protected areas, and the constitutional mandate to conserve the environment; limited skilled manpower, technology and capital; and the need for sustainable, value-added and equitable mineral development.', null, '[{"point": "Potential based on geology: Lesser Himalaya - dolomite, limestone, gypsum, coal, talc, quartzite, iron ore, graphite, slate, marble; Greater Himalaya/ leucogranite - tungsten, tin, beryl, rare metals, gemstones; metallic - copper, lead-zinc, gold, tungsten occurrences", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Known deposits/ status: dolomite (Pugli/ Samtse, Pemagatshel), gypsum (Pemagatshel - Khothakpa), limestone (Gomdar, Dungsum, Samtse - cement), coal (Samdrup Jongkhar), talc (Samtse), quartzite, iron ore (Chhukha?), marble/ stone; metallic largely unexploited, exploration by DGM/ GMC; Mines and Minerals Act/ policy 2017, state-owned mining (SMCL)", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Economic potential and challenges: export (dolomite, gypsum, ferrosilicon raw), cement, ferro-alloys, revenue; challenges - rugged terrain, access/ transport costs, environmental and social impacts, forest/ protected areas, small deposit size, limited exploration data/ skilled manpower, policy, landslides", "marks": 3}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation') and t.slug = 'geology'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-3'), 'standard', 10,
    1080, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (Technical, Geology): BCSE 2024, batch 1", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2024 Main Examination, Technical Paper III, Geology", "year": 2024, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/technical-paper-iii/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-technical-3-geo2024-case2', 4, null, null, (select id from public.specialisations where code = 'geology'), null)
  returning id
