Conclusion: summary of key findings and implications for future research or resource development. (5 marks)', 'The Himalayas formed through the collision of the Indian and Eurasian plates beginning about 50 million years ago, and continued convergence keeps them rising and seismically active. In Bhutan, all the main tectonic units are exposed - Siwalik, Lesser Himalayan, Greater Himalayan and Tethyan sequences - stacked along the MFT, MBT, MCT and South Tibetan Detachment, which gives the country complex geology, high earthquake hazard and widespread landslide-prone slopes. Bhutan''s mineral wealth lies mainly in industrial minerals (dolomite, limestone, gypsum, talc, quartzite, coal), with some potential for metallic minerals such as tungsten, copper, lead-zinc and gold.
Implications: further detailed mapping, exploration and research are needed to define resources and understand active faults; mineral development should be sustainable, value-adding and environmentally responsible; and geological knowledge must be used in planning hydropower, roads and settlements, and in earthquake and landslide risk reduction.', null, '[{"point": "Summary: Himalaya formed by India-Eurasia collision since ~50 Ma, still active; Bhutan exposes Siwalik, Lesser, Greater, Tethyan units separated by MFT, MBT, MCT, STD; high seismic and landslide hazard", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Implications: mainly industrial mineral potential with some metallic occurrences, need detailed exploration, sustainable mining with environment safeguards, hazard-sensitive planning, more research and monitoring", "marks": 2}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
